# Bounding a capacitor's discharge
#
# A sampled capacitor model supplies an initial voltage and an upper decay
# factor. N3 arithmetic rules derive voltage bounds at candidate steps and
# check when the tolerance is met.

@prefix : <https://eyereasoner.github.io/eyeling/examples/rc-discharge-envelope#> .
@prefix math: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/math#> .
@prefix log: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .
@prefix string: <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/string#> .

# The companion input supplies the decay bound. At each candidate index,
# raise that bound to the index and multiply by the initial voltage.
{
    :case :initialVoltage ?Initial ;
        :decayUpper ?DecayUpper ;
        :candidateStep ?Step .
    ?Step :index ?Index .
    (?DecayUpper ?Index) math:exponentiation ?Factor .
    (?Initial ?Factor) math:product ?Voltage .
} => {
    ?Step :upperVoltage ?Voltage .
} .

# Classify sampled upper voltages on either side of the strict tolerance.
{
    :case :tolerance ?Tolerance .
    ?Step :upperVoltage ?Voltage .
    ?Voltage math:lessThan ?Tolerance .
} => {
    ?Step :belowTolerance true .
} .

{
    :case :tolerance ?Tolerance .
    ?Step :upperVoltage ?Voltage .
    ?Voltage math:notLessThan ?Tolerance .
} => {
    ?Step :notBelowTolerance true .
} .

# Select a below-tolerance step whose supplied predecessor is not below.
# The first-step claim relies on those candidate and predecessor records.
{
    :case :maxStep ?MaxStep .
    ?Step :index ?Index ;
        :previousStep ?Previous ;
        :belowTolerance true .
    ?Previous :notBelowTolerance true .
    ?Index math:notGreaterThan ?MaxStep .
} => {
    :case :firstBelowToleranceStep ?Step .
} .

# Convert the selected index to seconds using the supplied sample period.
{
    :case :samplePeriod ?Period ;
        :firstBelowToleranceStep ?Step .
    ?Step :index ?Index .
    (?Index ?Period) math:product ?Time .
} => {
    ?Step :timeSeconds ?Time .
} .

# Print the supplied decay interval and the computed envelope result.
# The rules use the interval; they do not prove its bound on exp(-1/4).
{
    :case :exactDecaySymbol ?Symbol ;
        :decayLower ?Lower ;
        :decayUpper ?Upper ;
        :firstBelowToleranceStep ?Step .
    ?Step :index ?Index ;
        :timeSeconds ?Time ;
        :upperVoltage ?Voltage .
    (
        "# rc-discharge-envelope\n\n## Source files\n\n- [N3 rules](../rc-discharge-envelope.n3)\n- [Input TriG](../input/rc-discharge-envelope.trig)\n\n## Answer\nexact decay symbol : %s\ncertified decay interval : [%.10f, %.10f]\nfirst below tolerance step : %d\nfirst below tolerance time : %.3f s\nupper voltage at step %d : %.6f V\n\n## Explanation\nThe physical decay factor is exp(-1/4), but the example uses a finite double interval as the certificate. Because the interval lies strictly between 0 and 1, the capacitor voltage envelope contracts each sample. The upper envelope is the safety-relevant bound: once it falls below 1.0 V, every compatible exact trajectory is below tolerance."
        ?Symbol ?Lower ?Upper ?Index ?Time ?Index ?Voltage
    ) string:format ?Block .
} => {
    :report log:outputString ?Block .
} .
